WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.802
For time-poor adults, MoveRealm makes any room a movement adventure.

2
00:00:03.802 --> 00:00:08.364
This is a camera-free backup demo with synthetic narration and keyboard controls.

3
00:00:08.364 --> 00:00:11.786
No person appears, and no pose-performance result is claimed.

4
00:00:11.786 --> 00:00:17.869
The live agent evidence shown later uses a synthetic camera and is labelled as controlled evidence.

5
00:00:18.569 --> 00:00:24.193
The product is for a time-poor adult who wants light, low-impact movement without equipment or setup friction.

6
00:00:24.193 --> 00:00:28.163
It is not medical or rehabilitation guidance; stop if you feel unwell.

7
00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:31.471
The user chooses an energy level and a three-minute session.

8
00:00:31.471 --> 00:00:33.125
No jumping is locked on.

9
00:00:33.125 --> 00:00:40.733
They can scan their room for the full experience, or enter this resilient guided path when a camera or network service is unavailable.

10
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:45.688
In the live path, the user explicitly captures one room still.

11
00:00:45.688 --> 00:00:50.716
The local Node adapter sends that still to CodeBuddy, the runtime Movement Director.

12
00:00:50.716 --> 00:00:58.838
Here a synthetic camera contains no usable room, so CodeBuddy correctly returns an uncertain profile with only the centre lane permitted.

13
00:00:58.838 --> 00:01:04.253
The visible thirty-four point eight second badge is agent source latency, not movement-response latency.

14
00:01:04.253 --> 00:01:06.960
The temporary still is deleted after analysis.

15
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.449
The agent never gets the final word on physical safety.

16
00:01:11.449 --> 00:01:18.269
The user must confirm that the floor is clear, and can narrow or remove side steps before planning.

17
00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:21.679
The continue action stays disabled until that explicit confirmation.

18
00:01:21.679 --> 00:01:28.499
Unknown movements, jumping, plans outside the requested duration, and movements that conflict with these confirmed directions are rejected.

19
00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:36.334
A real camera run then calibrates framing with a T pose, an outward step, and a verified return to centre.

20
00:01:36.334 --> 00:01:38.474
MediaPipe runs in a browser worker.

21
00:01:38.474 --> 00:01:43.469
If confidence drops or the person leaves view, the game pauses instead of guessing.

22
00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:50.604
This backup uses the clearly labelled keyboard control option, so tracking frames per second and movement latency remain not applicable.

23
00:01:51.304 --> 00:01:54.082
The room becomes one polished Neon Rainforest.

24
00:01:54.082 --> 00:01:56.464
Reaches collect fireflies and grow branches.

25
00:01:56.464 --> 00:01:58.449
Squats shelter seedlings from rain.

26
00:01:58.449 --> 00:02:01.227
Permitted side steps redirect a glowing river.

27
00:02:01.227 --> 00:02:07.578
Each target has one cancellable lifecycle timer, and each movement must meet the displayed range envelope.

28
00:02:07.578 --> 00:02:10.753
In a real run, pose events stay on-device.

29
00:02:10.753 --> 00:02:16.707
In this guided run, the keyboard is a transparent judging control, not fabricated pose telemetry.

30
00:02:17.407 --> 00:02:19.720
After round one, the loop closes.

31
00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:26.275
Completion, observed movement range, tracking confidence when available, and the user's explicit difficulty answer become constrained telemetry.

32
00:02:26.275 --> 00:02:33.600
In this controlled live CodeBuddy example, only one of seven targets was reached and the user chose too hard.

33
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:42.854
The Movement Director reduced range from sixty to forty-five percent, tempo from point nine to point seven, and target rate from seven to five.

34
00:02:42.854 --> 00:02:48.251
The validator permits parameter tuning, but never lets the agent replace the known movement.

35
00:02:48.951 --> 00:02:53.437
The public guided build demonstrates the same product language with deterministic provenance.

36
00:02:53.437 --> 00:02:56.428
It visibly says Guided demo, not CodeBuddy live.

37
00:02:56.428 --> 00:03:02.035
Wide targets were missed, so the next envelope, tempo, and rate all change on screen.

38
00:03:02.035 --> 00:03:11.754
If the agent times out or returns malformed data, the production adapter uses this safe validated fallback rather than blocking the adventure or hiding the source.

39
00:03:12.454 --> 00:03:16.839
The experience ends with a garden postcard, completion, and honest runtime evidence.

40
00:03:16.839 --> 00:03:20.858
Three fifty-two second movement rounds equal two point six active minutes.

41
00:03:20.858 --> 00:03:25.608
Two twelve second rests bring the complete adventure clock to exactly three minutes.

42
00:03:25.608 --> 00:03:29.993
Keyboard tracking is shown as N A, not thirty frames per second.

43
00:03:29.993 --> 00:03:34.012
The proof row also states that live video was never uploaded.

44
00:03:34.712 --> 00:03:36.806
Here is the technical loop.

45
00:03:36.806 --> 00:03:40.995
One approved still crosses the browser boundary for scene analysis.

46
00:03:40.995 --> 00:03:47.277
CodeBuddy proposes a scene, a three-round quest, and later adaptations through an asynchronous Node adapter.

47
00:03:47.277 --> 00:03:54.397
Zod contracts enforce the room directions, exact duration, no jumping, three known movements, and conservative fallback behavior.

48
00:03:54.397 --> 00:03:57.329
Separately, live frames remain inside the browser.

49
00:03:57.329 --> 00:04:04.868
A MediaPipe worker produces thirty-three landmarks, the movement state machines emit validated events, and Phaser renders immediate feedback.

50
00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:13.664
Telemetry returns only after a round, and another safety boundary validates the changed parameters before the next world state is applied.

51
00:04:13.664 --> 00:04:19.946
The build approach was vertical-slice first: reliable input and one complete round before agent integration.

52
00:04:19.946 --> 00:04:28.323
A practical CodeBuddy tip is to request strict JSON schemas and one repair prompt, then validate again outside the model.

53
00:04:29.023 --> 00:04:38.706
The defensible idea is the closed loop: understand the actual room, constrain the plan, turn movement into play, observe the result, and visibly adapt.

54
00:04:38.706 --> 00:04:45.968
The public guided demo and source repository are ready, along with controlled open, tight, and uncertain room evidence.

55
00:04:45.968 --> 00:04:54.440
Real-person frame rate, visible response latency, time to first movement, and three user trials are still marked pending rather than estimated.

56
00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.072
MoveRealm: turn any room into an adaptive movement adventure.
